1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,000
Hi, if you're like me, you spent hours scrolling on TikTok or Pinterest looking at Minecraft building ideas without the intent to ever actually build them.

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:12,000
Now, I don't know about you, but sometimes inspiration just randomly hits.

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:15,000
And I have to play Minecraft. Like, that's all that's on my mind.

4
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:22,000
And lately I've been seeing a whole lot of cottagecore Minecraft content circulating around the internet, getting a lot of views.

5
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:28,000
And so I downloaded some shaders and launched Minecraft at like 6 FPS and built this for nostalgia.

6
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:34,000
And if you're like literally any person who's played Minecraft over the years, these houses were the bomb.

7
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:36,000
They were like move-in ready.

8
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:40,000
Of course, there was only room for like three things to fit in there.

9
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:42,000
And then there was this problem.

10
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:48,000
It's Minecraft cottagecore aesthetic because the mushroom houses with this aesthetic are like next level.

11
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:50,000
They are chef's kiss. They are immaculate.

12
00:00:50,000 --> 00:01:00,000
And suddenly your wiki how tutorial on how to make simple mushroom houses featuring acacia plank flooring are more an aesthetic than the 2013 mustache trend.

13
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:08,000
And if this strangely specific scenario applies to you, then boy will you like this tutorial.

14
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:13,000
So now I present three ways to make mushroom houses in Minecraft.

15
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:16,000
Yay.

16
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:19,000
So the first house is pretty easy.

17
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:26,000
You're going to want to start out with a statistically impossible mushroom in the middle of a field and use bone meal to make it big.

18
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:31,000
Now stare at that mushroom until you decide to mutilate it to look aesthetic like this.

19
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:36,000
I wanted the mushroom to look a bit different than the other simple mushroom houses you typically see.

20
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:41,000
So I went for a more of a bell-shaped curve for the top of the mushroom.

21
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:45,000
You could also use one of those mini water bottles for reference.

22
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:50,000
I modeled mine based on an upside down flower pot and I think it turned out pretty good.

23
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:59,000
Once you have achieved maximum upside down flower pot conformity, then you can start modifying the stem to make it look stalkier.

24
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:03,000
I used the mushroom stem block and smooth quartz stairs.

25
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:09,000
And if you're in survival, you'll probably want to mine a few of the big mushrooms with a silk touch axe to get the materials for this.

26
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:12,000
I used birch plank flooring to match the rest of the interior.

27
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:16,000
Now, I really like this house, but there's not much room.

28
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:19,000
Sorry for my spore sense of humor.

29
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:22,000
Here's the big grand reveal of this house.

30
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:25,000
Very simple, I give it a 8 out of 10.

31
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:27,000
Next house!

32
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:34,000
This is a little bit more difficult than the last one, and so you're going to start out by making a frame that looks like the outline of a handmade cube of play-doh.

33
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:37,000
The kind that the edges never seem sharp enough.

34
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:43,000
And then start sculpting it to look like the body of this absurdly priced plastic mini mouse teapot I found on Amazon.

35
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:48,000
I used smooth quartz blocks, slabs, and stairs to accomplish this.

36
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:51,000
And if you want to use mushroom stem blocks, I'm sure you could.

37
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:57,000
But I just like quartz because it makes the mushroom stem seem smooth and continuous, sort of like a differentiable equation.

38
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:00,000
Also because it's not a pain to get all those stems.

39
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:04,000
After this, start making the stem taller to look almost like a BB-8.

40
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:07,000
And then give the wannabe BB-8 some helicopter blades.

41
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:10,000
These will be the radius of your circular mushroom cap.

42
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:14,000
Then connect those little helicopter blades to form a circle.

43
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:17,000
Also, I never really found these graphs useful.

44
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:19,000
I always find them on like TikTok.

45
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:24,000
But then again, I always just wing it and it doesn't look very circular, so maybe they're helpful to you.

46
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:26,000
But mine turned out pretty great in the end.

47
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:29,000
Anyway, start outlining the circle in red mushroom blocks.

48
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:31,000
And then be indecisive.

49
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:33,000
And then decide on making the easy way out.

50
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:37,000
And make a circular, spherical looking mushroom cap.

51
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:39,000
Mine is about 6 blocks tall.

52
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:43,000
And unlike me, you probably want to make sure there's no weird blocks left exposed.

53
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:47,000
Then I made the door and the window and outlined them in the most superior wood type.

54
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:48,000
Spruce wood.

55
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:52,000
I used planks, fences, and stairs as well as oak leaves for the window boxes.

56
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:57,000
To add some extra whimsy, you can make a dilapidated cobblestone chimney and drape that with oak leaves.

57
00:03:57,000 --> 00:04:04,000
To add some stuff to the interior, I connected the chimney to a fireplace furnace type thing.

58
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:07,000
And then added an unnecessary amount of bookshelves and leaves.

59
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:10,000
Because when in doubt, add oak leaves and bookshelves.

60
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:11,000
That's my motto.

61
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:15,000
Also, I used flower pots, turtle eggs, and sea pickles for the inside.

62
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:21,000
And then I discovered that in 1.16.5, you can place turtle eggs on flower pots, which is kind of weird.

63
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:26,000
Anywho, I used vines as a ladder to the attic area, which will be a cute little bedroom.

64
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:30,000
I outlined the roof with spruce wood and then placed some bookshelves behind them.

65
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:33,000
This would also be a cool enchanting room, but the house demands a bedroom.

66
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:38,000
So I added in a skylight, which looks good with the shaders, and I'm sure it would look good without them.

67
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:40,000
So this is the finished build.

68
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:43,000
It only took me about 45 minutes to do in creative, so not too bad.

69
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:46,000
Ta-da!

70
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:50,000
Right, on to the last one.

71
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:54,000
This one is kind of hard, but not like PryorGaming at the end of his TikToks kind of hard.

72
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:59,000
You're going to want to start out with a circular base like such, and then add a small circle attached to it.

73
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:03,000
Once you have your two little circle buddies, start making them taller and rounder.

74
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:07,000
I focused on the bigger circle first because it's going to be the centerpiece for the build.

75
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:12,000
Also, if this video gets 100 likes, I'll make a video of my little sister following this tutorial to see how she does,

76
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:15,000
because she's bad at building and I'm bad at explaining things.

77
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:17,000
Okay, back to the tutorial.

78
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:19,000
If you don't know what shape to make the base of the big mushroom,

79
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:23,000
I referred to the gourd from the 2007 Disney classic, Secret of the Magic Gourd,

80
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:25,000
and mine turned out pretty alright.

81
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:29,000
Also, I need to know, was anyone else low-key traumatized by that movie?

82
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:33,000
Because when I first saw that movie, I guess I just thought gourds were strange pumpkins,

83
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:37,000
so for the longest time, I was fundamentally confused on what gourds were.

84
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:41,000
So anyway, you're going to start making another one of those helicopter circle things,

85
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:46,000
and here's a better explanation using calculus if you want to know how to make circles in Minecraft.

86
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:49,000
Presenting. Building circles. Yay!

87
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:52,000
So you're going to want to start out with this sort of graph paper.

88
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:57,000
Just trace something circular onto it, and then fill in the blocks that the circle crosses.

89
00:05:57,000 --> 00:05:59,000
Make sure it all has the same radius.

90
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:01,000
And then this is where the calculus comes in.

91
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:04,000
If you look at tangent lines, you know what tangent lines are, I hope.

92
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:06,000
You probably don't.

93
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:15,000
But the slope of the tangent line is sort of the slope of if you were to draw the slope through the grid squares of the circle.

94
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:17,000
This made a lot more sense inside my head.

95
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:22,000
You can trace pretty much any circle on the graph paper and repeat this process.

96
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:26,000
Just make sure the radius is the same and that each quadrant is the same.

97
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:28,000
And there you go, you have a circle in Minecraft.

98
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:33,000
Okay, now moving on to our other little mushroom buddy.

99
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:37,000
You're going to want to make him a wonky little mushroom that sort of stems off from the big one.

100
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:40,000
It's really hard to explain how I did this, but I would recommend using organic shapes

101
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:43,000
and have the top of the mushroom thinner than the base.

102
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:45,000
Then add a tiny little cap.

103
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:50,000
For the top of the big mushroom, I basically did the same thing as I did for the medium tutorial.

104
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:53,000
And despite it being vastly bigger, the cap is about the same height.

105
00:06:53,000 --> 00:06:55,000
Maybe even five blocks this time.

106
00:06:55,000 --> 00:06:59,000
I kind of wanted to make it sort of squishy like the first build,

107
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:02,000
but I lost all my brain cells thinking about calculus.

108
00:07:02,000 --> 00:07:06,000
If you want the mushroom to be super cottagecore, you can add some birch leaves that hang like vines

109
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:08,000
and some hanging lanterns.

110
00:07:08,000 --> 00:07:10,000
You could also use shroom lights.

111
00:07:10,000 --> 00:07:13,000
I also added a dilapidated chimney to match the vibe.

112
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:17,000
And then for the interior, I started again by connecting the fireplace to the chimney

113
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:20,000
and built a loft area with a ladder leading up to it.

114
00:07:20,000 --> 00:07:24,000
And then I got distracted by a wandering trader and quickly resumed my work.

115
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:28,000
And I used an absurd amount of birch leaves to decorate.

116
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:32,000
If you can't tell from my Pinterest page, birch leaves are my favorite.

117
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:36,000
Also, if you're wondering how they move in the wind, that's the shaders, and I use BSL shaders.

118
00:07:36,000 --> 00:07:39,000
As far as I know, they only work for Java Edition.

119
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:42,000
I made a little kitchen space in front of one of the windows,

120
00:07:42,000 --> 00:07:46,000
complete with some cake and some turtle eggs, and then added some seating by the fire.

121
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:50,000
In front of the other window, I made what HGTV calls a breakfast nook,

122
00:07:50,000 --> 00:07:52,000
complete with some plants.

123
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:54,000
While the bedroom space is smaller than the previous build,

124
00:07:54,000 --> 00:07:57,000
I think it turned out nicely and I wouldn't mind living there.

125
00:07:57,000 --> 00:08:01,000
After decorating the bedroom, I remembered there was a bunch of empty space in the loft,

126
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:05,000
which I promptly decorated with barrels because that can be practical

127
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:07,000
when they're not converting all the villagers to fishers.

128
00:08:07,000 --> 00:08:11,000
And I did notice I accidentally made a sort of shrine to Pinocchio, which is cool, I guess.

129
00:08:11,000 --> 00:08:15,000
On the outside, I was going for a house-ish entrance,

130
00:08:15,000 --> 00:08:20,000
but I ended up making it more like a hobbit hole with spruce trapdoors and slabs and stairs.

131
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:24,000
I also added some dark oak for better contrast,

132
00:08:24,000 --> 00:08:28,000
and ended up expanding the windows downward because it just looked better.

133
00:08:28,000 --> 00:08:33,000
I added some shutters to the bedroom window and shroom lights to better illuminate it at night.

134
00:08:33,000 --> 00:08:37,000
There was something that was still missing, though, and I couldn't decide what to do,

135
00:08:37,000 --> 00:08:42,000
so I tried this thing I saw on TikTok where you make mushrooms out of endrods and mushroom blocks,

136
00:08:42,000 --> 00:08:46,000
which I was a bit skeptical of, but I really liked how they turned out.

137
00:08:46,000 --> 00:08:51,000
So yeah, those are three mushroom houses you could easily build in Minecraft.

138
00:08:51,000 --> 00:08:53,000
Comment below which one you'd live in.

139
00:08:53,000 --> 00:08:58,000
I know I definitely would pick the second one because of the skylight, but that's just me.

140
00:08:58,000 --> 00:09:03,000
And if you enjoy this content and would like to see more dollar store green tutorials,

141
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:05,000
please feel free to hit subscribe.

142
00:09:05,000 --> 00:09:07,000
See y'all later, bye!


